    "title_en": "The Courage to Ask for Help: Sharing What We Have Carried",
    "title_zh": "開口求助的勇氣：扛了一整年，從分享開始",
    "summary_en": "After a long year, the hardest sentence may be a simple admission that things have been difficult. This finale looks at the courage to ask for help, shares what the JC JoyAge project tells us gently, and looks back over the series.",
    "summary_zh": "扛了一整年，最難的也許只是說一句「我不太好」。這篇以賽馬會JoyAge的資料為起點，溫柔地談開口求助的勇氣，也回望整個系列，邀你回到 /culture-daily 繼續新的一天。",
    "body_en": "<p>Near the end of the year, a man takes the long way home from work. He walks past the harbour, where the lights are bright and the air is cool, and stops on a bench to look at the water. At dinner that evening, his family will ask how he has been, and he will say, as he usually does, that he is fine. This is a common pattern in Hong Kong and in many places: the easy answer is one word, while the honest answer takes more courage than the moment seems to allow.</p>\n<p>This final article in the Culture Desk series is about that courage — the courage to ask for help, and the equally quiet courage to share what we have been carrying. It is a gentle subject, and it deserves gentle handling. The facts that follow come from a project that works with older people in Hong Kong. They are not here to shock. They are here to remind us that behind every ordinary evening on a bench or at a dinner table, there may be a person who would welcome being asked a second time.</p>\n<h2>What the JC JoyAge project tells us</h2>\n<p>The Hong Kong Jockey Club Charities Trust supports a project called JC JoyAge. According to information published by the Trust in 2025, about 10% of Hong Kong elderly show depressive symptoms. The same source reports that the suicide rate for people aged 60 and over is 18.7 per 100,000 — the highest of any age group. These are serious facts, and they should be met with care rather than alarm.</p>\n<p>The project's information also says that depressed elders are 2.2 times more at risk of suicide. Since 2016, it has supported more than 28,000 people through 54 non-governmental organisation service units. Those last numbers are worth sitting with. They describe a response built over years, through many small teams, rather than a single dramatic intervention. In many households, the change that helps is similarly small: a regular phone call, a neighbour who notices, a question asked at the right time.</p>\n<h2>Holding the facts lightly</h2>\n<p>It would be easy to turn figures like these into a story of crisis. That is not the intention here. A percentage is not a person, and a rate is not a life. The 10% figure tells us that low mood among older people is not rare; it does not tell us who those people are or what they need. The 18.7 per 100,000 figure tells us that the risk in this age group is the highest of any; it does not give us a name or a face. Facts can point us toward compassion, but they cannot replace it.</p>\n<p>So consider them as a reason to look more closely at ordinary life. Imagine an elderly neighbour who has stopped coming down for morning exercise. Imagine a grandfather who answers the phone less often than he used to. Imagine an aunt who jokes about being forgotten, and means it more than the joke suggests. These are imagined scenes, not reports about anyone. They are the kind of small changes that a person who cares might notice — and that a person in difficulty might hope someone would notice.</p>\n<h2>Why asking is hard</h2>\n<p>In many Hong Kong families, asking for help is difficult because the family is expected to manage its own problems. There is a quiet belief that a good son or daughter should cope, that a person should not burden others, and that difficult feelings are best kept private. A common pattern is a relative who says they are tired and leaves it there, and a family who accepts the answer because it is impolite to push. Everyone in that scene may be acting from kindness, and still the person remains alone with what they feel.</p>\n<p>Imagine trying to say that you have not been well to someone who loves you, and worrying that you will make them worry. Imagine being the person on the other side, sensing that something is wrong, and not wanting to pry. This is the gap that help has to cross. It is not crossed by one grand conversation. It is crossed by small, repeated invitations: a call at the same time each week, a walk together, a sentence that says you can tell me and I will not make a fuss.</p>\n<h2>What sharing actually looks like</h2>\n<p>Sharing what we carry does not always mean a formal confession. Often it looks like ordinary company. It is the daughter who sits with her mother over a cup of tea and lets the silence be comfortable. It is the neighbour who knocks on the door on a rainy afternoon, with no particular reason. It is the friend who says that they have had a hard week too, and thereby makes it safe for the other person to agree. These gestures are small, and in the context of the JC JoyAge figures, they are not small at all.</p>\n<p>The project's record — more than 28,000 people supported through 54 service units since 2016 — is a reminder that help works best when it is steady and close to home. Service units are places people can return to, where the same faces are there again. A family can imitate that. A regular call, a standing invitation to dinner, a relative who checks in without waiting for a crisis: these create the same steady presence on a smaller scale. The point is not to become a professional. It is to become reliably present.</p>\n<h2>The courage to begin</h2>\n<p>If there is one practical idea to take from this, it is that the first sentence does not have to be perfect. Saying that you have not been feeling like yourself lately is enough. Asking whether the two of you can talk for a minute is enough. Saying that you think you could use some help is enough. In many situations, people who are struggling fear that they must explain everything clearly before they are allowed to be helped. They do not. A beginning can be untidy and still be real.</p>\n<p>It also helps to remember that asking for help is not a burden placed on others. It can be a gift. When a person shares what they have carried, they give the people around them a chance to be useful, to feel trusted, and to practise the kind of care they may one day need themselves. In a city that often values self-reliance, allowing someone to help can be its own quiet act of generosity. It says: I trust you enough to let you see the difficult part.</p>\n<h2>Looking back over the series</h2>\n<p>Across these Culture Desk mornings, we have walked through many rooms of Hong Kong life. We have looked at the flat that decides how a family shares space, at the red packet that carries more than money, at the helper who holds a household together, and at love that survives in the gaps of a timetable. We have considered the hours of night work, the messages that travel too far in a family group, and the debts that are carried in silence. Each subject was ordinary, and each held more feeling than first appeared.</p>\n<p>Now, at the close of the series, the thread connecting them is simple: beneath the routines of a crowded city are people trying to be seen, to be fair, and to be kind to one another in small, workable ways. The Culture Desk is not finished; new mornings will be added at /culture-daily. Come back when you have a little time, and bring these questions with you. Until then, notice the person near you who has been carrying something quietly — and let them know, in one plain sentence, that you are glad they are there.</p>\n<h2>💭 A question to carry into tomorrow</h2>\n<p>Over this series, which ordinary moment in Hong Kong life surprised you most — and who is one person you could thank or check in with today for the part they have carried?</p>",
